  "read": {
    "headline": "Core PCE printed 3.0%. The long end rose anyway.",
    "standfirst": "Wednesday's data were all one way: ADP 90,000 against 70,000, core PCE +0.2% on the month and 3.0% on the year against 3.3%, final Q2 GDP revised up to 2.2%, Chicago PMI 58.8 against 51.2. The front end took the soft inflation print and October's hike odds fell to about 35–40%; the long end took the strong growth prints and the ten-year and thirty-year rose again, closing a September in which the ten-year added about 50bp, its worst month since 2022. Stocks gave up a morning gain to close the quarter mixed — the S&P 500 down 0.25% to 7,651.54, the Dow down 0.86%, the Nasdaq up 0.24% — and Micron beat and guided above consensus after the close. The November Brent contract expired at $103.53 and December is now the only price, $96.95 this morning. Overnight the Nikkei rose 3.24% on the chipmakers, the Tankan held at 24, the yen fell to 158.3 on a cautious BoJ summary, and China shut for Golden Week. Eight Fed speakers and ISM manufacturing today, payrolls tomorrow.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The split curve is now a month old. Soft inflation lowered the bill rate; strong growth raised the bond.",
        "text": "The three-month bill is 4.20% from 4.25% on Tuesday, the two-year 4.88% from 4.89%, the ten-year 5.29% from 5.26% and the thirty-year 5.64% from 5.59%, with the twenty-year at 5.68% the highest point on the curve. Two-tens is 41bp from 37bp, bill-to-ten 109bp from 101bp, the widest of the cycle. In the funds the 1–3 year Treasury rose 0.05% and municipals 0.82% while the 20-year-plus fund fell 0.58% and is now down 12.89% over twelve months, 9.04% over three; investment-grade credit lost 0.22%, emerging-market bonds 0.48%. The month's numbers are the ones to keep: the ten-year rose about 50bp in September, its largest monthly rise since September 2022, and Bloomberg's global bond index lost 2.4%, its worst quarter since late 2024. A 3.0% core PCE and a 2.2% GDP arrived on the same morning and the curve priced both, at opposite ends."
      },
      {
        "title": "Technology was the only sector up on the day, in the month and in the quarter. Micron explains why.",
        "text": "Technology rose 0.64% on Wednesday while staples fell 1.52%, health care 1.35%, industrials 1.27% and financials 1.13%; the Nasdaq gained 0.24% as the Dow lost 0.86%. September's column: technology +6.59% is the only sector positive on the month, utilities −7.33%, real estate −7.11%, financials −6.64% and materials −6.47% the worst, with the S&P 500 ETF flat at +0.11%. Over three months technology is +8.39% and energy +15.56% while utilities are −13.81% and industrials −9.21%. After the close Micron reported revenue of $54.23 billion, up 379% and above the $51.3 billion expected, earnings of $33.42 a share, a gross margin of 87%, and guided the current quarter to $60–63 billion against $57.4 billion — then traded little changed, the stock already up 273% this year. In the factors, growth beat the index by 0.17% and value lagged by 0.22%; momentum is 6.91% ahead over a month, the small-cap value fund 4.88% behind. The VIX closed at 16.34."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent's row is clean now: the November contract has expired and December is the only price.",
        "text": "November Brent settled its last day at $103.53, up 1%, and WTI at $90.42, up 1.2%; this morning December Brent is $96.95, down 1.10%, and WTI $89.19, down 1.36%. The five-day column's −7.06% on Brent is the roll, not a sell-off, and today is the first session on which every front-month chart shows the gap; WTI, which did not roll, is down 3.48% on the week. Brent gained about 14% in September and WTI 5%, and the one-month column — Brent +2.43%, WTI −1.14% — has the two benchmarks going different ways on the Saudi re-routing. Kpler counts at least 16.5 million barrels a day leaving the Gulf excluding Iran in the first four weeks of September, matching pre-war volumes by different routes, and Goldman now has the market near balance with Brent at $85 by year-end; JPMorgan says exports are at 98% of pre-war but it cannot model the endgame. Refined product exports are the gap, at roughly half of pre-war: heating oil is $4.64, up 117.43% this year and 45.78% over three months, and gasoline stocks are at a 12-year low. Natural gas fell 1.62% and is 6.85% lower on the week."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brazil and Japan rose; Europe fell more than 1% everywhere; the quarter column is Korea's.",
        "text": "Brazil rose 2.14% and Japan 0.98% on Wednesday while Korea fell 2.31%, Turkey 2.73%, France 1.55%, Italy 1.44%, Spain 1.42%, Germany 1.41% and Switzerland 1.37%; the developed ex-US fund lost 0.62% and the emerging-markets fund 0.91%. The month's column is where the dollar went: Turkey −15.08%, Argentina −9.72%, Indonesia −9.28%, South Africa −8.00%, France −6.25%, Mexico −6.04%, India −5.85%; Korea +3.97%, Taiwan +2.88% and Japan +2.35% are the three large markets that rose. Over three months Korea is still +44.81% and Taiwan +57.42% against the S&P 500 ETF's +16.39%; over twelve months Korea is +123.20%. This morning the Nikkei is up 3.24% near 68,900 on Advantest, Tokyo Electron and SoftBank after Micron, the Kospi up about 1.6%, Taiwan up 0.78%, while Australia is down 1.77% and Hong Kong flat; Shanghai is shut until 8 October. Europe's Wednesday losses — Stoxx 600 −0.4%, CAC −0.89% — were the quarter-end flow the country ETFs show in dollars."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar index closed September at a three-month high. The yen gave back Wednesday's gains overnight.",
        "text": "EUR/USD is 1.13, down 2.32% over a month and 3.67% on the year, after the euro's 2.5% September fall, its largest since July 2025 — the euro-area inflation prints that should have helped it (Germany 3.3%, Italy 4.1%, Spain 5.0%) did not, because October's ECB odds halved to about 30% anyway. The dollar is 3.06% stronger on the franc over a month and 6.36% on the peso, which lost 2.27% on the week; the kiwi is 4.57% weaker over a month. USD/JPY is 158.34, up 0.64% on the day after touching 157.34 as the BoJ summary of opinions showed government caution on the next hike, though still down 1.14% over a month and 1.71% over three — the only major the dollar lost to in the quarter. The won is 11.68% stronger over three months. The Australian dollar sits at 70 cents after a A$495 million trade surplus, the smallest in years. In crypto Bitcoin is $84,173, up 0.73% and 36.90% over three months while down 29.06% over twelve; Cardano rose 2.67%, Chainlink is +86.96% over three months and Litecoin is 7.27% lower on the week."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold rose 0.77% on the lower bill rate. Cotton fell its limit for a second day.",
        "text": "Gold is $4,218.90, up 0.77% on the day with silver up 1.89% to $61.71 and platinum 1.22% — the relief came from the front end of the curve again, a three-month bill at 4.20% instead of 4.25%, and the Bank of Korea said it will start buying gold in December. The month is still red: gold −4.04%, silver −5.60%, platinum −1.50%; over three months silver is −18.89% and over twelve +29.43%. Copper rose 0.24% to $6.64 and is +0.56% over a month and +35.94% over a year; aluminium fell 1.26% and is 5.87% lower on the week. In the crops, cotton fell 4.53% for a second straight limit session and is 13.75% lower over a month, cocoa lost 0.76% and 18.32% over a month, corn is 5.56% lower on the week and wheat 13.64% lower over a month. Sugar, +6.34% on the week, and coffee, +4.34%, are the two rows going the other way. Lean hogs are −29.70% over three months."
      }
    ]
